# SABIN Gallery Row

A **Craft CMS predefined row**, not a page. Self-contained and reorderable among other
rows; it must never assume anything about what sits above or below it.

**Start with `PROJECT-STATE.md`** — current state, how to run it, what is still open.

Templates are authored against Craft's real API and a local harness fakes those calls, so
the template never learns it isn't in Craft. That is deliberate: Phase 3 is a data-source
swap, not a rewrite. Do not add local indirection for convenience — it becomes something
Foster has to unpick.

## Never violate

- **Crawlable content is the product.** Every unique tile's alt text, copy, CTA label and
  `href` must be in the served HTML without JS. JS may enhance; it must never be the only
  path by which tile content reaches the DOM.
- **Real anchors only.** No routable tile is a `<button>` or a click-handled `<div>`.
  Build 2.0 uses `<button>` for its cards — this is the one place not to copy it.
- **Tile links are siblings, never nested.** Whole-tile link plus corner Build icon, each
  with its own distinct accessible label. Nested `<a>` is invalid HTML.
- **No duplicate DOM ids.** Each unique tile appears once. Client-generated padding repeats
  are inert: no `data-tile-id`, `aria-hidden="true"`, outside the tab order.
- **One layout engine.** Swiss and masonry are the same dense-packed CSS Grid; they differ
  only in whether row spans vary. Never build a second, column-based engine.
- **Two-column floor.** Exactly two columns at 320px, never one. The gutter drives both the
  grid `gap` and the `minmax()` floor from a single custom property — if they diverge, the
  floor silently breaks.
- **Settings live on the row.** Every gallery setting reads off `row.*`, is Craft-driven,
  and is never visitor-facing. No code-side defaults: a missing field is a validation
  error, so that no setting can live outside the row.
- **Reduced motion is absolute.** No momentum scroll, no tile transitions, no motion
  autoplay under `prefers-reduced-motion`. Inertia disabled must mean *no* scroll
  interception at all — keyboard, scrollbar drag and find-in-page behave natively.
- **Brand tokens only.** No free-form colour, size or spacing value anywhere. Text-tile
  options are named tokens; free values are rejected at validation.
- **Square tiles, never upscaled.** Media crops 1:1 through ImagerX honouring focal point.
  Undersized sources render native and get flagged — never stretched.

## Stack

Twig + Tailwind CSS v4 + Alpine.js. PHP/Composer for the local harness only.

**No React. No JS templating language or static-site generator in place of Twig** — either
turns the Phase 3 handoff into a rewrite. Build 2.0 is a separate React app; the gallery
links out to it per-tile and shares no code.

GSAP ^3.13.0 is available site-wide if a motion need genuinely calls for it. Momentum
scroll is deliberately ~40 lines of vanilla rAF, because guaranteeing mid-glide retargeting
and zero interception when disabled matters more than using a library.

## Conventions to follow, not reinvent

`predefined row reference.twig` is Foster's production row. Match it: a single root
element, fields read off `row`, theming through the shared colour macro, named typography
classes from the existing scale, ImagerX for all transforms and srcset, small UI pieces as
component includes.

`templates/_macros/color.twig` is a **local stand-in**, deleted at handoff when Foster's
real macro takes over. Never hardcode colour classes around it.

## Before changing anything

- `docs/architecture/gallery-row.md` — the design and its reasoning
- `docs/craft-field-definitions.md` — the handoff contract; changing field shapes changes
  what Foster has to build
- `CLAUDE.md` — the full principle set

`Instructions for Gallery.md` is the **superseded** original brief. Good for *why*; not
current on *what*.

## Verify, don't assert

`bin/preview` to run it. `bin/preview --with-siblings` proves row independence — nothing
about the gallery may change. Tests live in `tests/html/` and `tests/browser/`. Report
measured numbers rather than claiming something looks fine.
